 *     did. The session is a normal Checkout Session, so Stripe's existing
 *     webhook + `classifyCheckoutSession` + `fulfilCheckoutSession` pipeline
 *     fulfils it completely unchanged.
 *   - PayPal is a genuine second rail (lib/paypal.ts, real Orders API) —
 *     `@paypal/react-paypal-js`'s `<PayPalButtons>` creates a PayPal order via
 *     our own server action, then captures it server-to-server on approval.
 *   - Coinbase Commerce is a genuine third rail — clicking it creates a charge
 *     server-side and redirects to Coinbase's own hosted charge page (there is
 *     no embeddable Coinbase Commerce widget), returning here once paid.
 *   - PayPal and Coinbase both funnel into `fulfilExternalPayment`
 *     (lib/fulfil-checkout.ts), the SAME dispatch Stripe's webhook uses.
 *
 * This component is presentational + payment-orchestration only: pricing,
 * promo/coupon holds and provisioning all happen server-side, exactly as they
 * did before this UI existed. Every number shown here is a PROP, never computed
 * in this file, so the wizard's existing pricing math remains the single source
 * of truth (see each wizard's own dueCents/cardCents/walletApplied derivation).
